        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThis change is a faithful, idiomatic mirror of the existing `RequireCityCapacity` / `CityAtCapacityException` / `CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler` triplet, which is clearly the established pattern for \"reject-a-create-with-a-status\" rules in this codebase.\n\n- **Logic in the right unit.** The count-and-reject check lives in its own `Require*` function class, matching the one-responsibility-per-step convention. The exception carries the datum (the limit), and the handler owns the HTTP mapping \u2014 the same split as every sibling in `escalation/`.\n- **Correct pipeline placement.** `dailyLimit` is wired *after* `DefaultOwnerRegistrationDate` (so `registrationDate` is populated) and *before* `build`/`save` (so no self-count), and the class javadoc documents exactly that dependency. It sits alongside the other `Require*` guards, which is where a maintainer would look.\n- **No new catch-all or duplication.** The `findAll().stream().filter(...).count()` shape repeats the existing `RequireCityCapacity` idiom rather than inventing a new one; that repetition is the codebase's own style, not duplication introduced here.\n- **Conventions honored.** Escalation YAML named by FQCN, `govern: [ transaction ]` on the new step consistent with its neighbors, checked exception extending `Exception`, and the handler's `Map.of(\"errors\", List.of(...))` shape matching the sibling handlers.\n\nNaming (`RequireDailyOwnerLimit`, `DailyOwnerLimitExceededException`), javadoc style, and the `LIMIT` constant all align with the surrounding code. Nothing structural to improve."
